 leader in this work  in her research she talks about these  two different types of political  engagement institutional and  participatory and this really reflects  these overall changes we're seeing in  the world in terms of how our phones and  our connectivity changed pretty much  everything this is how it works for  particularly youth in the Civic space so  I'm gonna really really simplify overly  simplify this to take you through what  these mean first institutional  engagement I have my bring on the  fundraising emails here institutional  engagement is kind of your traditional  approach to digital organizing that a  lot of social movement organizations  pursue it's really driven by the idea of  the marketing funnel it's top-down  there's only a few calls to action it  doesn't do a good job supporting local  action they're just like let's push all  this stuff out and then hopefully we'll  get a few people to make a donation and  actually looks was looking at my emails  from all the organizations that I follow  in Wisconsin and in the month in  November over let's say I think over 55%  of them the only call to action was to  donate no I wanted to just have like a  response to be like I'm a grad student  no on the other hand we've got  participatory engagement and this is  very different typically more popular  among younger people and this is much  less like a funnel and more like a  ladder that uses popular culture to  onboard people into movements and one of  the organizations that Henry Jenkins  writes a lot about it in this space is  called the Harry Potter Alliance which  basically uses the fictional world of  Harry Potter to get people engaged in  politics all over the world and there's  a few different features of this  participatory engagement that I kind of  identified in looking at a lot of the  work on this space first these forms of  engagement tend to be remixable they  tend to be very diverse so it could be a  really  locate a difficult thing it could be a  really easy thing and there's a lot of  different ways to engage they tend to be  modular which means like that Imperial  Walker costume I can look at a picture  of that and I can find a way to  replicate that and potentially repurpose  it for different causes and they're also  personalizable so I can kind of put my  stamp and use it as a way to express  something about Who I am and so I think  overall and organizing we really need  both of these approaches we need both  those like these funnels and these  ladders because we live in a diverse  world and we need diverse ways to engage  but what I've seen is that right now in  the technology space there are very very  few tools that support more  participatory forms of engagement so I  did an analysis of over 20 different  tools that are used both by existing  social movement organizations and by  activists and I generally just found  that a lot of these technologies are  very one-way they again focus on a  limited set of calls to action they're  just generally not aligned with this  more participatory form of engagement  and so overall in looking at the tools  that exist in this space I kind of  noticed these three trends one is that  very very few of them if any of them  really support local action at the  municipal in the county level the second  is there's not a ton of collaboration  that is supported in these tools usually  an organization will maybe put together  an app to support their cause  but then they don't end up getting a lot  of users and it's not maintained very  well and all of these other issues  happen and and it just kind of ends up  getting ignored and beat by being  collaborative I think that you can I'll  talk about this more but you can get  more people involved and you can do a  better job supporting the technology and  then finally just what I've been talking  about they're not particularly  participatory so the next section I'm  going to look at game design and how we  can use it as a tool to drive more of  this type of participatory engagement  so the first thing here is I talked in  the in the thesis a lot about the idea  of game design thinking and this is my  definition of game design thinking and  approach to the design of civic  technology that uses the structure of a  game to model complex social problems in  a way that makes them easier for  community members to understand and  engage with this is different from other  definitions both Ben Stokes and your  Gordon used this idea in slightly  different ways and then I think are also  very useful but this is kind of how I  how I was using it how do we use games  to think about the complex structure of  the social movement field and make it  easier to understand how you can plug in  so this is really essentially the second  chapter of my thesis and it's organized  around the resident games framework  which is in Resident games which is from  the education arcade here and they  identify these kind of four principles  of of learning learning games and they  are under the whole learner under the  sociology of learning a play the deep  connection between content and game and  the learning context so I'm just gonna  very briefly go through each of these  sections in this chapter a couple of the  takeaways that I found certainly not an  exam  so first of all I rephrase this one is  playing and learning how do we look at  the connection between the content in  the game and in resident games they say  if you're asking how do I make this  topic fun you're asking the wrong  question what you really want to do is  try to find what's already fun about a  particular process and then connect  players to that so what I was thinking  about social movements again this pulls  me back to this idea of creative  activism what is already fun about  taking part in a grassroots movement and  then the other thing in this part that I  wanted to highlight is the idea of  scaffolding so you know in learning  theory we talked about the zone of  proximal development which is basically  how do we keep people kind of just at  the edge of their comfort zone so that  they can can be continually learning  through an experience experience and  what I see in a  lot of the particularly digital forms of  websites for participating with large  social movement organizations is that  they don't really scaffold engagement  very well a lot of the time that's one  example you might go on you might say  I'm interested and they'll ask you to  tweet something is the first step like  okay I can I can do that and then after  that it says you know next step and the  next step is start a local chapter which  is like this massive as great if I go  find other people there's nothing in  between and I think that this idea of  scaffolding even if it was just this  whole loan could be really helpful in  designing civic technologies that can  support that type of engagement second I  talk about identity and expression this  kind of speaks to the idea that learners  are not you know an empty vessel we  already have things that we know and we  need to be able to build off and connect  to those things and there's there's four  pieces here the first is we need to make  personal expression a mechanism of  achieving the desired engagement goal  again for in my case I think this  relates to creative activism second  contribution creates ownership and  belonging so Sasha talks a lot about  this in their book on transmedia  organizing and talking about how  creating a you know a video or telling  your story through some form of media  really increases your sense of  connection to a particular movement  third I think participatory culture very  well fits in with this how do we  personalize what we're sharing and what  we're doing and then finally we needed  to support spread ability so you can't  just build for one platform it's got to  be able to things have got to be able to  be shared  next collaboration and community this is  another thing that I think we could  really learn from when we look at Civic  technologies in general but specifically  for the organising space and there's  again four things here the first is we  need to support reputation and  reciprocity so the ability for people to  engage and build relationships over time  to be affirmative with low barriers to  entry its prints pretty self-explanatory  promote  peer learning and training and promote  collaboration so the the two key points  here are with collaboration again this  kind of speaks to that logic of  connective action how do we create tools  that can support a large number of  issues and causes and enable people to  connect and say hey like you know I  follow three fifty.org because I care  about climate change but I didn't know  about like the Sun rise movement and all  of these other things that I might also  be doing and in terms of peer learning  when I was an undergrad I studied with  Constance 10 cooler who does a lot of  work on World of Warcraft and how people  in the game of World of Warcraft learn  from and teach each other and there's a  really there's really interesting  customs that exist in that game and in  many other games that can show us how  players more advanced players can be  mentors for new players and I think that  there's also something to that that  could be really useful for an experience  for onboarding people into the space of  organizing and then finally strategy and  context again lore on or the learning  context so first this really for me is  what got me is just start thinking about  location-based games is a useful medium  for for the problem I was looking at  because they promote habits of  connection to place and what I mean by  that is really demonstrated while in  research on the game called Mogi which  was the precursor to pokemon go and what  the researchers found in looking at  players of that game is that the players  will actually like throughout the course  of their day change where they go in  order to interact with different sites  in the game and when I started playing  Pokemon go I noticed that I was doing  the same thing you're like oh that's  statue outside the lab like I got to  stop by there I'm not going to go this  way because I want to play over here and  I think there's something about that  like visual cue of a space that you see  that could be really useful for a civic  engagement game especially if it's  focused on local issues which also  relates to the idea of situating  learning and engagement so if there's  something I want you to know about the  library can I put that information at  the library  and then finally in this section I also  thought a lot about knowledge games and  specifically crowdsourcing so one of the  big challenges of you know information  systems at the local level is there's  tons and tons and tons of local elected  representatives so are there ways that  we can build it into the game to  actually collect information about who  holds those positions and what their  stances are on different issues what  information do we need to know at the  local level and can we incentivize play  around learning that so thinking about  all of that I'm gonna just spend a  couple minutes here taking you through  the game itself which is called forward  again this was built for the city of  Appleton Wisconsin that's a seventy five  thousand person City so that's just a  little bit south of Green Bay  it's tends to be pretty conservative  although there's an interesting mix of  organizations there and first I just  want to touch a little bit on on  Wisconsin context so I think that when  we think of Wisconsin there are certain  things that come to mind which are all  very well represented on our state  quarter we have a cow and we have cheese  and corn but one thing that a lot of  people don't know is that our state  motto is forward and Wisconsin has a  really strong tradition of organizing of  activism and of progressive ideas and  this isn't typically reflected in a lot  of our politics in the state's today but  it's something that I'd like to be able  to remind people of and so that's kind  of why I chose this name forward is  thinking and also because I want people  to actually think forward think about  the future as a way to kind of get past  our more partisan barriers so forward is  a location-based civic engagement game  that can extractivist with Action Alerts  called missions based on their interests  skills availability and location  throughout the game players collect  elements to complete missions with other  players in support of local causes and  completing these missions allows players  to submit and vote on headlines  about the state's future so this is  gonna be launching a pilot of this three  five weeks on a web app starting on July  4th so wish me luck in building that  over the next month and the hypothesis  is that games can facilitate structured  cross move and collaboration between  organizations and their supporters  thereby increasing both issue awareness  and personalization of calls to action  at the local level and so first I wanted  to define what are some of my goals and  creating this experience the number one  goal is to promote creative activism I  want to expand how people think about  civic engagement so if I can get  somebody to do one piece of chalk art or  make one meme and tag it in a location  in the city like I consider that a win  other things of course are just about  learning about local government and  local civics and then again thinking  positively about the future shifting the  frame of how we're thinking about these  issues so the game's story is that we  are trying to collaboratively write a  futuristic edition of the local paper  for May 29 2014 1 since 200th birthday  and the way this works is that as you  play the game negative headlines which  are sourced crowd-sourced from the  community show up and then you have to  counteract them as a player by putting  in positive headlines and you've got to  get enough likes on your positive  headlines that they beat out the  negative ones  so the first step when you join this  game is you actually create a little  avatar this is part of that identity  formation expression piece something  that we actually found was really really  popular in paper prototyping this is a  little Avatar card that one of the early  players filled out and we randomly  picked this like aesthetic of these cute  little animals with like superhero stuff  like superhero costumes and I just kind  of like continued going with that  because people seem to like it  and so this is kind of the over the mock  up sort of promo thing of the game  you're trying to run around and collect  these different elements and it's all  it's all place-based  so the different components of the game  itself first we have tactics tactics are  templates for creative civic engagement  so if anybody's familiar with  Instructables I really like the way that  they do this  um step by step how did you create your  Imperial Walker  you know what materials did you need and  I want to start with about 50 different  tactics in the game and then  participants can also add their own and  those tactics exist in three different  categories which are guided by our three  little non player characters here we've  got the honey badger for advocacy the  dragon pug for a creative and nerdy corn  for research so these represent  different types of tactics that you can  engage with and you can level up in each  of these categories as you go through  the game next campaigns so this is kind  of what you think of what our local  organizations trying to achieve I'm  gonna be sourcing these campaigns from a  minimum of 15 different organizations  and I just want to know you know are you  trying to get a you know get a certain  number of signatures on a petition pass  a local resolution what tangibly are you  trying to succeed or achieve and how can  our players through creative activism  support that so missions are how they do  that missions combine a tactic and a  campaign and enable people to run out  into the community and do something to  raise awareness about that thing it  could be something that you see somebody  doing like actually dressing up or doing  a flash mob or it could be just them  digitally tagging and geo-locating a  digital piece of media for other players  to find which they'll be part of the  test to see what works with that story  is just document the completion of  missions those are the things that you  like in order to get points for the  headlines which of course is how you  influence the overall story and again I  want this to be something that can serve  multiple organizations these are the  five different issues that the game  looks at and so we'll be working with  hopefully I'm in a moment of three  organizations on each of these issues to  define  the sort of pre initial set of missions  and you can also do team play I won't  get into how that works but teams if  I've done people are supported to  hopefully see if that's something that  other people enjoy doing and so with  that I just want to say thank you first  of all to Scott and Sasha my thesis  advisors too you know Wisconsin United  to amend all the people that I worked  with in Wisconsin the city of Appleton  the Appleton Public Library and of  course all of the CMS faculty and my  cohort who's been really really amazing  I think I learned somehow I learned more  here than I could have possibly imagined  and I'm just extremely grateful to have  spent the last two years with you all  thank you  [Applause]  so  yeah so I think the question is  generally about who is this for  and the short answer is help to learn  that this summer was I piloted the the  key audiences that I'm targeting are  people who are already following an  organization but maybe aren't very  involved for